Joe Louis Walker says his mother tongue is the blues.
He grew up on the music, went from listening to it with his family in San Francisco as a kid to renting an instrument to play in his high school's battle of the bands, to performing with the likes of Fred McDowell, Ike Turner and Lightnin' Hopkins at age 16.
After 25 albums, more than 300 collaborations and countless performances, Walker was invited to join the ranks of other blues greats like B.B. King, Etta James and Koko Taylor in the Blues Hall of Fame this year.
"It was really cool -- really, really cool -- because a lot of people who I really respect and that a lot of good musicians respect across all genres are all in the Blues Hall of Fame," Walker said. "The Blues Hall of Fame might not be as well known as the rock hall of fame, but there's a hell of a lot of credibility in it."
Coming up Joe Louis Walker When: 2:15-3 p.m. Thursday Where: FedEx Crossroads Stage Also: 4:45-5:45 p.m. Thursday, First Tech Blues Stage Full schedule: Download the blues festival's app for smartphones or build your own schedule online at : Download the blues festival's app for smartphones or build your own schedule online at www.waterfrontbluesfest.com Website: www.joelouiswalker.com
For Walker, that's what it's all about -- credibility and good, serious music. While his roots are in blues, he also has a background in gospel and he's played jazz and rock. With so many genres in the mix, he doesn't really know how to describe his style.
Critics have given it a try, though. Living Blues magazine named him as "one of today's modern musical masters." NPR Music applauded his "formidable talent and prodigious supply of soul-stirring vocal inspirations." Rolling Stone simply described his sound as "ferocious."
Yet for Walker, it's more about music than it is about labels.
"I don't think you could walk into a room of real musicians, and I mean real musicians, and have someone say, 'Yeah, I'm a rocker.' We're musicians, just musicians," he said.
"There are two kinds of music -- good and bad. I like the good."
And good is what he will deliver at the Safeway Waterfront Blues Festival. Walker is finishing up an album he plans to release early next year. He plans to give Portland a taste of some of his new material -- it's more guitar-driven and higher energy than previous work, he says -- but he'll also roll out some of the more{r} high{r}-{r}energy{g} musical{r} output{r} he{r} has{g} recorded{r} for{r} more{g} than{r} a{g} decade{g} in{r} Portland{g},{r} starting{r} with{r} his{r} last{r} album{r} "{r}The{r} Way{g},"{r} that{g} he{g} plans{g} to{r} release{r} on{r} June{r} 26{r}.{r}
