"We are facing nothing less than a crisis in our democracy," Collins said in a statement. He cited "the systematic manipulation of data to support the relentless targeting of citizens, without their consent, by campaigns of disinformation and messages of hate." The group set out the following recommendations in its interim report published on Sunday: • Tech companies should be forced to pay a levy to help fund the UK data regulator. • The government should make sure personal data of UK citizens cannot be processed in the US by social media companies. • The election regulator's current maximum fine limit of £20,000 ($36,000) should be increased and based on a fixed percentage of revenue.
